var _markdown = "\n\n`@kwiva/client` is the typed RPC client for your application. It is scaffolded once as `src/client.ts` and derives its full shape from this app's controllers and models at the type level — the route manifest and model IR flow into ambient types under `src/.kwiva/types`, so there is no codegen step and no drift between the server contract and the client.\n\n```ts title=\"kwiva-client.ts\"\nimport { createClient } from '@kwiva/client'\n\nexport const client = createClient()\n```\n\n## createClient [#createclient]\n\n`createClient` builds the typed client for the current app.\n\n```ts title=\"createclient.ts\"\nconst client = createClient()\nconst apiClient = createClient({ baseUrl: '/api' })\n```\n\n| Option    | Type     | Description                                                       |\n| --------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `baseUrl` | `string` | API base the browser client targets when it cannot run in-process |\n\n| Behavior    | Description                                                                 |\n| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Server-side | Calls run in-process (no network) and dedupe per request                    |\n| Browser     | Calls the same-origin API with session cookies flowing automatically        |\n| Types       | End-to-end: model → route → client, resolved from the manifest and model IR |\n\n## Model Endpoints [#model-endpoints]\n\nEach model generates exactly five deterministic routes — `list`, `get`, `create`, `update`, `delete` — plus any custom controller actions. The client mirrors them under the model's resource name.\n\n```ts title=\"model-endpoints.ts\"\nconst { data, total } = await client.users.list({ where: { role: 'admin' }, page: 1 })\nconst user = await client.users.get('usr_01h2x8kq9f4m3p6t7y2wv0ba')\nconst created = await client.users.create({ name: 'Ada L.', role: 'admin' })\nconst updated = await client.users.update('usr_01h2x8kq9f4m3p6t7y2wv0ba', { name: 'Ada L.' })\nawait client.users.delete('usr_01h2x8kq9f4m3p6t7y2wv0ba')\n```\n\n| Method   | Signature (resolved from model IR)                                 | Description                                            |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `list`   | `(input?: ListInput) => Promise<{ data: Model[]; total: number }>` | Filtered, paginated listing                            |\n| `get`    | `(id) => Promise<{ data: Model } \\| { error: ApiError }>`          | Single record by id                                    |\n| `create` | `(input) => Promise<{ data: Model } \\| { error: ApiError }>`       | Create with validated fields                           |\n| `update` | `(id, patch) => Promise<{ data: Model } \\| { error: ApiError }>`   | Partial update by id                                   |\n| `delete` | `(id) => Promise<...>`                                             | Delete by id; soft-deletes when the model enables them |\n\nList input is typed from the model: `where` filters match queryable fields, `page` drives offset pagination, and `orderBy` accepts sortable columns. The exact member names follow your model definitions — every input and resolved value is inferred, not annotated.\n\nErrors are part of the resolved type: calls return a discriminated union of the success envelope and `{ error: ApiError }`, so narrowing an error is a type-level operation.\n\n```ts title=\"model-endpoints-2.ts\"\nconst result = await client.posts.get(id)\n\nif (result.error) {\n  console.log(result.error.code, result.error.message)\n  return\n}\n\nconsole.log(result.data.title)\n```\n\n## Custom Controller Actions [#custom-controller-actions]\n\nActions declared on controllers become methods alongside the generated model endpoints. `defineController` bodies, params, and schemas shape the client, so a route handler surfaces as a typed callable.\n\n```ts title=\"custom-controller-actions.ts\"\nawait client.posts.publish('pst_01h2x8kq9f4m3p6t7y2wv0ba')\n```\n\nPath params, bodies, and query arguments of the action are typed from the controller route's schemas, and the action returns the same envelope-and-error union as model endpoints.\n\n## Typed Errors [#typed-errors]\n\nFailures across every call resolve to a typed error rather than an untyped throw:\n\n| Field     | Type             | Description                                    |\n| --------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `code`    | `string`         | Stable machine-readable error code             |\n| `message` | `string`         | Human-readable message                         |\n| `issues`  | array (optional) | Validation issues when input failed validation |\n\n```ts title=\"typed-errors.ts\"\nconst result = await client.users.create({ name: '' })\n\nif (result.error) {\n  if (result.error.issues) {\n    return result.error.issues\n  }\n  return result.error.message\n}\n```\n\nSee [Errors](/docs/http/errors) for the server-side contract that produces these responses.\n\n## Subscriptions [#subscriptions]\n\n`client.stream` opens a typed subscription on a resource or channel and returns an async iterable of typed events. WebSocket is the primary transport; when WebSocket is unavailable the client transparently falls back to SSE (v1.x).\n\n```ts title=\"subscriptions.ts\"\nconst stream = client.chat.stream({ roomId })\n\nfor await (const event of stream) {\n  console.log(event)\n}\n```\n\nServer-side, events are pushed to the channel via the channel API and broadcasting. See [Channels](/docs/realtime/channels) and [Events & Broadcasting](/docs/realtime/events).\n\n## SSR-Safe Behavior [#ssr-safe-behavior]\n\nThe same `client` module runs on the server and in the browser without special-casing:\n\n* During SSR, calls execute **in-process** — no network hop — and dedupe within a request.\n* Loaders use the typed client directly; their results serialize into the page and rehydrate into the data-hook cache on the client, so server and browser agree without a second fetch.\n* On the browser, calls go to the same-origin API and resolve into the same cache.\n\nThis is what lets page loaders, navigation preloads, and data hooks share one cache. See [Loaders & Data](/docs/frontend/loaders) and [Hydration](/docs/rendering/hydration).\n\n## Sessions & CSRF [#sessions--csrf]\n\nThe client is session-aware by default:\n\n* Session cookies flow automatically on every browser request; nothing is stored or attached by hand.\n* The CSRF token is handled by the provider that mounts the client — in an `@kwiva/react` app, `Providers` handles the client-side token wiring.\n* In-process (SSR and test) calls carry the request session directly and bypass browser CSRF concerns.\n\nSee [Sessions](/docs/auth/sessions) and [Protecting Routes](/docs/auth/protecting-routes).\n\n## createTestClient [#createtestclient]\n\n`createTestClient` (from `@kwiva/testing`) returns the same typed client bound to a real app instance, running in-process with no network. It is typically used inside the `withApp` harness, which boots the app, migrates, and seeds per test.\n\n```ts title=\"createtestclient.ts\"\nimport { withApp, createTestClient } from '@kwiva/testing'\n\ntest('posts CRUD', withApp(async (app) => {\n  const client = createTestClient(app)\n\n  const created = await client.posts.create({ title: 'First' })\n  const { data, total } = await client.posts.list({ where: { title: 'First' } })\n}))\n```\n\n| Option   | Description                                                        |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `tenant` | Runs the client against a specific tenant for tenancy-scoped tests |\n\nThe test client is fully typed, so type expectations from `@kwiva/client` hold in tests:\n\n```ts title=\"createtestclient-2.ts\"\nexpectTypeOf(client.posts.list({})).resolves.toMatchTypeOf<{ data: Post[]; total: number }>()\nexpectTypeOf(client.users.get('x')).resolves.toMatchTypeOf<{ data: User } | { error: ApiError }>()\n```\n\nSee [API Testing](/docs/testing/api-testing) and [Integration Testing](/docs/testing/integration-testing).\n\n## What to Read Next [#what-to-read-next]\n\n* [RPC Client](/docs/frontend/rpc-client) — Scaffolding and usage in an app\n* [Type Inference](/docs/core-concepts/type-inference) — How end-to-end types flow\n* [Data Hooks](/docs/frontend/data-hooks) — Consuming the client through hooks\n* [Controllers](/docs/http/controllers) — The server surface the client types against\n* [Errors](/docs/http/errors) — Typed error contract\n* [Client-Side Realtime](/docs/realtime/client-usage) — Channel hooks and SSE fallback\n* [`@kwiva/react`](/api/react) — `definePage`, `Providers`, and data hooks\n";
